(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-2-anatomy-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"Anatomy Comprehensive Master Exam — Form A","category":"Anatomy","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"What structures make up the integumentary system?","choices":["Only skin and hair, without any glandular or nail components","Skin, hair, nails, sweat glands, oil glands, mammary glands, and enamel of teeth","Only sweat glands and oil glands, excluding skin, hair, and nails entirely","Skin and the skeletal system, sharing a common connective tissue origin"],"answer":1,"exp":"The integumentary system is composed of skin, hair, nails, sweat glands, oil glands, mammary glands, and enamel of teeth. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What are the five functions of the skin listed in the material?","choices":["Only heat regulation, maintained through sweat glands and superficial blood vessels","Only protection and sensation, via the epidermis and dermal nerve endings","Protection, containment, heat regulation, sensation, and synthesis/storage of vitamin D","Digestion and absorption of nutrients, aided by specialized epidermal enzymes"],"answer":2,"exp":"The skin's functions are protection (from environmental effects), containment (of tissues/organs, preventing dehydration), heat regulation (via sweat glands/blood vessels/fat), sensation (via nerve endings), and synthesis/storage of vitamin D. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What are the two main layers of the skin, and what characterizes each?","choices":["Epidermis (superficial cellular layer, tough protective outer surface) and dermis (basal regenerative and pigmented connective tissue layer)","Epidermis (deep connective tissue layer) and dermis (superficial cellular layer), reversed from their actual positions","Subcutaneous tissue and deep fascia, forming the two main structural layers described in this material","Only one layer: the epidermis, since the dermis is classified as an entirely separate connective tissue organ"],"answer":0,"exp":"The skin consists of the epidermis, a superficial cellular layer creating a tough protective outer surface, and the dermis, a basal (deep) regenerative and pigmented connective tissue layer. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What is the epidermis composed of, and is it vascular?","choices":["A keratinized simple (single-layer) epithelium; highly vascular throughout its thickness","A keratinized stratified epithelium with a tough outer surface of keratin; avascular, nourished by vessels in the underlying dermis","Smooth muscle tissue; avascular, relying on diffusion from the underlying dermis","A single layer of connective tissue; highly vascular, supplying nutrients directly"],"answer":1,"exp":"The epidermis is a keratinized stratified (layered) epithelium with a tough outer surface composed of keratin; it is avascular and nourished by vessels in the underlying dermis. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"How often is the epidermis renewed, per the material?","choices":["Never — epidermal cells do not turn over","Every 5 years","Every 24 hours","Every 25-45 days"],"answer":3,"exp":"The outer layer of the epidermis is continuously shed or rubbed away, with replacement of new cells from the basal layer, renewing the entire epidermis of the body every 25-45 days. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What are the five layers of the epidermis, from top (superficial) to bottom (deep)?","choices":["Stratum corneum, stratum lucidum, stratum granulosum, stratum spinosum, stratum germinativum/basale","Stratum corneum, stratum basale, stratum granulosum, stratum lucidum, stratum spinosum","Only three layers exist: corneum, spinosum, and basale","Stratum basale, stratum spinosum, stratum granulosum, stratum lucidum, stratum corneum (reversed order is correct top to bottom)"],"answer":0,"exp":"The five layers of the epidermis, from top (superficial) to bottom (deep), are the stratum corneum, stratum lucidum, stratum granulosum, stratum spinosum, and stratum germinativum/basale. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is the stratum germinativum/basale, and what cell types does it contain?","choices":["A layer found only in the thick skin of the palms and soles, containing several rows of dead, clear cells lacking nuclei","A single layer of stem cells firmly attached to the basal lamina, composed of basal cells and melanocytes (ratio 4:1 to 20:1), refurbishing keratinocytes","The most superficial epidermal layer, containing only dead keratin and no living or dividing cells of any kind","A layer of dead, flattened cells with no nucleus, forming the outermost protective barrier against water loss"],"answer":1,"exp":"The stratum germinativum/basale is a single layer of stem cells firmly attached to the basal lamina, separating the dermis from the epidermis; it is composed of basal cells and melanocytes in a ratio of 4:1 to 20:1, and it refurbishes keratinocytes (also containing Merkel cells). — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What do Merkel cells in the stratum basale do?","choices":["Produce lamellar granules","Present antigens to T cells","Stimulate sensory nerve endings","Produce melanin, coloring nearby keratinocytes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Merkel cells, found in the stratum basale, stimulate sensory nerve endings. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What is the stratum spinosum, and what cell type initiates the immune response there?","choices":["Located above the stratum spinosum, only a few cells thick, containing mostly melanocytes connected by desmosomes; Merkel cells (about 6%) initiate the immune response","Located above the stratum lucidum, several cells thick, containing mostly corneocytes connected by desmosomes; dendritic cells (about 6%) initiate the immune response","Located above the stratum corneum, a single cell thick, containing mostly melanocytes connected by tight junctions; mast cells (about 10%) initiate the immune response","Located above the stratum basale, several cells thick, containing mostly keratinocytes connected by tonofibrils; Langerhans cells (about 6%) initiate the immune response"],"answer":3,"exp":"The stratum spinosum is located above the stratum basale, several cells thick, containing mostly keratinocytes connected to each other by tonofibrils, with some melanocytes and Langerhans cells (about 6%) that initiate the immune response. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is produced in the stratum granulosum, and what effect does keratohyalin have?","choices":["Keratohyalin and keratin are produced; keratohyalin coats cells with a lipid-rich substance making the layer water-resistant and causing the top layer of cells to die","Only lipids are produced; lamellar bodies coat cells with a protein-rich substance making the layer flexible and causing the bottom layer of cells to die","Filaggrin and collagen are produced; filaggrin coats cells with a keratin-rich substance making the layer elastic and causing the entire layer to regenerate","Sebum and keratin are produced; sebum coats cells with a water-rich substance making the layer permeable and causing the middle layer of cells to proliferate"],"answer":0,"exp":"The stratum granulosum, located above the stratum spinosum, is where keratohyalin and keratin are produced; keratohyalin granules coat cells with a lipid-rich substance, making the layer water-resistant and causing the top layer of cells to die (this layer also forms calluses). — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Where is the stratum lucidum found, and what are its cells like?","choices":["Found only in the axillae and groin folds, covering the stratum corneum; cells retain a nucleus, are rounded and loosely packed, filled with lipid droplets oriented perpendicular to the surface","Found throughout thin skin of the face and trunk, covering the stratum spinosum; cells have a single nucleus, are cuboidal and loosely packed, filled with melanin granules scattered randomly","Found in thick skin of the palms and soles, covering the stratum granulosum; cells have no nucleus, are flattened and densely packed, filled with keratin filaments oriented parallel to the surface","Found only in the scalp and eyebrows, covering the stratum basale; cells have multiple nuclei, are columnar and sparsely packed, filled with tonofibrils oriented vertically to the surface"],"answer":2,"exp":"The stratum lucidum is found in thick skin of the palms and soles, covering the stratum granulosum; its cells have no nucleus, are flattened and densely packed, and are filled with keratin filaments oriented parallel to the surface. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What characterizes the stratum corneum, and how long does it take keratinocytes to travel there and be shed?","choices":["Most superficial layer, 15-30 layers of flattened keratinized cells, water-resistant but not waterproof; keratinocytes take 15-30 days to arrive and are shed in about 2 weeks","The deepest epidermal layer, a single cell thick, fully waterproof and impermeable; keratinocytes take 5-7 days to arrive and are never shed throughout life","A layer found only over the anterior eye surface, 3-5 layers of nucleated cells, fully permeable to water; keratinocytes take 1-2 days to arrive and are shed within hours","A layer that never sheds cells throughout life, 40-50 layers of cuboidal cells, entirely waterproof; keratinocytes take 60-90 days to arrive and accumulate permanently"],"answer":0,"exp":"The stratum corneum is the most superficial layer of the skin, consisting of 15-30 layers of flattened, keratinized cells; it is relatively dry, water-resistant but not waterproof (allowing perspiration), with keratinocytes taking 15-30 days to travel from the basal layer to this layer and being shed in about 2 weeks. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What are the medial and lateral canthus, and the palpebral fissure?","choices":["Canthus connects the eyelids at the corners of the eye; palpebral fissure is the area between the eyelids","Canthus is the area between the eyelids; palpebral fissure connects the eyelids at the corners of the eye","Neither structure is related to the eyelids; both instead describe the boundaries of the bony orbit","Both terms describe the same structure, referring collectively to the entire eyelid margin"],"answer":0,"exp":"The medial and lateral canthus connect the eyelids at the corners of the eye, while the palpebral fissure is the area between the eyelids. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What do eyelashes contain, and what reflex do they trigger?","choices":["Contain lacrimal gland tissue, triggering pupil constriction","Contain sebaceous glands only, with no nerve association","Contain the root hair plexus, which triggers the blinking reflex","Contain the conjunctiva, triggering tear production"],"answer":2,"exp":"Eyelashes contain the root hair plexus, which triggers the blinking reflex. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What is the conjunctiva, and what are its two subdivisions?","choices":["Epithelial lining of the eyelids/eye surface; subdivided into palpebral conjunctiva (inner eyelid lining) and bulbar/ocular conjunctiva (outer eye lining)","A gland producing tears; subdivided into lacrimal and tarsal glands lining the upper and lower eyelids","A muscle moving the eye; subdivided into the rectus and oblique groups controlling gaze direction","The colored part of the eye that regulates pupil size; subdivided into the iris proper and the sphincter and dilator pupillae muscles"],"answer":0,"exp":"The conjunctiva is the epithelial lining of the eyelids and outer eye surface, with fluid production helping prevent it from drying out; it is subdivided into the palpebral conjunctiva (inner lining of the eyelids) and the bulbar (ocular) conjunctiva (outer lining of the eye). — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What do tarsal glands secrete, and what happens if a tarsal gland becomes infected?","choices":["Secrete mucus for the nasal cavity; infection causes chronic sinus congestion","Secrete tears; infection may result in excessive, uncontrolled tearing","Secrete a lipid-rich product keeping eyelids from sticking together; infection may result in a cyst","Secrete aqueous humor; infection has no clinical significance for the eyelid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Tarsal glands secrete a lipid-rich product that keeps the eyelids from sticking together (located along the inner margin of the eyelids); an infection of the tarsal gland may result in a cyst. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What structures make up the lacrimal apparatus, and what is its overall pathway?","choices":["Only the lacrimal gland, with no duct system, secreting tears directly onto the corneal surface without drainage","The cornea, iris, and pupil, which work together to focus incoming light and regulate the amount reaching the retina through the pupil's aperture","Lacrimal glands (produce tears), lacrimal canaliculi, lacrimal sac, and nasolacrimal duct; tears flow over the ocular surface then into the nasal cavity via the nasolacrimal canal","Tarsal glands, glands of Zeis, and the conjunctiva, which jointly produce and distribute the lipid layer of tears"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lacrimal apparatus consists of the lacrimal glands (which produce tears), lacrimal canaliculi, lacrimal sac, and nasolacrimal duct (canal); tears flow over the ocular surface and into the nasolacrimal canal, which enters the nasal cavity — explaining why heavy crying causes a \"runny\" nose. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which cranial nerve innervates the lateral rectus muscle, and which innervates the superior oblique?","choices":["Lateral rectus: CN IV; Superior oblique: CN VI","Neither muscle is innervated by a cranial nerve","Both are innervated by CN III","Lateral rectus: CN VI; Superior oblique: CN IV"],"answer":3,"exp":"Per the \"LR6, SO4, all the rest are 3\" mnemonic, the lateral rectus is innervated by CN VI, and the superior oblique is innervated by CN IV. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which cranial nerve innervates the superior rectus, inferior rectus, medial rectus, and inferior oblique muscles?","choices":["CN III","CN IV","CN VII","CN VI"],"answer":0,"exp":"CN III innervates the superior rectus, inferior rectus, medial rectus, and inferior oblique muscles (\"all the rest are 3\"). — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What movement does the superior oblique (SO) produce, and what movement does the inferior oblique (IO) produce?","choices":["SO: down and out; IO: up and out","SO: up and out; IO: down and out","SO: in; IO: out","Both muscles produce identical movement"],"answer":0,"exp":"The superior oblique (SO) produces movement down and out, while the inferior oblique (IO) produces movement up and out. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What movement does the medial rectus (MR) produce, and what movement does the lateral rectus (LR) produce?","choices":["MR: up; LR: down","Both muscles produce the same movement","MR: in; LR: out","MR: out; LR: in"],"answer":2,"exp":"The medial rectus (MR) moves the eye in (medially), while the lateral rectus (LR) moves the eye out (laterally). — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What are the three tunics of the eye, and what does each correspond to?","choices":["Fibrous tunic (sclera/cornea), vascular tunic (iris/ciliary body/choroid), and neural tunic (retina)","Fibrous, vascular, and neural tunics all describe different regions of the sclera alone","Only two tunics exist: fibrous and neural, with vascular supply distributed diffusely throughout both","Fibrous tunic (choroid), vascular tunic (retina), and neural tunic (sclera)"],"answer":0,"exp":"The three tunics of the eye are the fibrous tunic (making up the sclera and cornea), the vascular tunic (including the iris, ciliary body, and choroid), and the neural tunic (also called the retina). — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What does the fibrous tunic (outer layer) of the eye make up, and what is its function?","choices":["Makes up the iris and ciliary body; regulates the amount of light entering the pupil","Makes up the sclera and cornea; provides protection and attachment sites for extra-ocular muscles","Makes up the retina; converts light into neural signals sent along the optic nerve","Makes up the lens only; focuses incoming light directly onto the retina's surface"],"answer":1,"exp":"The fibrous tunic (outer layer) makes up the sclera and cornea, providing some degree of protection and attachment sites for the extra-ocular muscles; the cornea is described as modified sclera. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What does the vascular tunic (middle layer) of the eye consist of, and what are its functions?","choices":["Consists only of rods and cones; converts light to neural signals sent along the optic nerve to the visual cortex","Consists of the lens only; focuses light onto the retina by changing curvature during accommodation","Consists of blood vessels, lymphatics, and intrinsic eye muscles; regulates light entering the eye, secretes/reabsorbs aqueous fluid, and controls lens shape","Consists only of the sclera and cornea; provides protection only, with no role in fluid regulation"],"answer":2,"exp":"The vascular tunic (middle layer) consists of blood vessels, lymphatics, and intrinsic eye muscles; it regulates the amount of light entering the eye, secretes and reabsorbs aqueous fluid, and controls the shape of the lens, including the iris, ciliary body, and choroid. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"How many chambers does the heart consist of, and what are the two circuits it pumps blood into?","choices":["Four chambers; pumps into only the pulmonary circuit, with systemic circulation handled separately","Two chambers; pumps into only the systemic circuit via a single ventricular pump","Three chambers; pumps into the coronary and pulmonary circuits simultaneously","Four chambers (two atria, two ventricles); pumps into the pulmonary circuit and systemic circuit"],"answer":3,"exp":"The heart consists of four chambers (two atria and two ventricles) and pumps blood into two circuits: the pulmonary circuit and the systemic circuit. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What two layers does the pericardial membrane form, and what reinforces the parietal pericardium?","choices":["Visceral pericardium (epicardium) and parietal pericardium; the parietal pericardium is reinforced by the fibrous pericardium","Visceral and parietal pericardium, both reinforced by the myocardium's outer muscular fibers","Endocardium and myocardium, reinforced by a thin layer of areolar connective tissue","Only one layer: the epicardium, reinforced directly by the fibrous pericardium beneath it"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pericardial membrane forms two layers: the visceral pericardium (also called the epicardium) and the parietal pericardium; the parietal pericardium is reinforced by a layer called the fibrous pericardium. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What is the normal amount of pericardial fluid, and where is it contained?","choices":["No fluid is normally present within the pericardial sac at rest","500-1000 mL, contained within the heart chambers themselves","5 mL, contained within the myocardium's muscular layer","15-35 mL, contained between the visceral and parietal pericardial layers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Pericardial fluid, a type of serous fluid, is normally present in an amount of 15-35 mL, contained between the visceral and parietal pericardial layers. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What are the functions of the pericardium?","choices":["Only produces hormones, such as atrial natriuretic peptide, in response to stretch","Only conducts electrical impulses between the atria and ventricles","Only pumps blood through the coronary vessels during diastole, independent of ventricular contraction","Protects and anchors the heart, prevents overfilling with blood, and allows friction-free heart movement"],"answer":3,"exp":"The pericardium protects and anchors the heart, prevents overfilling of the heart with blood, and allows the heart to work in a relatively friction-free environment. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What are the three layers of the heart wall, from external to internal?","choices":["Endocardium, myocardium, epicardium","Epicardium, myocardium, endocardium","Pericardium, myocardium, endocardium","Only two layers: myocardium and endocardium"],"answer":1,"exp":"The heart wall consists of three layers, from external to internal: the epicardium (external surface), the myocardium (consisting of cardiomyocytes), and the endocardium (internal surface). — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What are intercalated discs, and what two types of cell junctions do they contain?","choices":["A type of heart valve, unrelated to cell junctions, located between the atria and ventricles","Specialized cell-to-cell junctions between cardiac cells, containing desmosomes (binding actin) and gap junctions (allowing ion movement between cells)","Found only in skeletal muscle, not cardiac muscle, connecting adjacent myofibrils end to end","A single type of junction: tight junctions only, sealing adjacent cardiac cells completely and preventing any ion exchange between them"],"answer":1,"exp":"Intercalated discs are specialized cell-to-cell junctions in cardiac cells, containing desmosomes (primarily desmoglein 2, binding actin of adjacent cells) and gap junctions (allowing ions to move directly from one cell to another, letting all sarcomeres contract as one unit). — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What is the sarcolemma, and what wraps each muscle layer of the heart?","choices":["Sarcolemma is a type of heart valve; muscle layers are wrapped in epithelial tissue lining the endocardium","Sarcolemma is unrelated to cardiac muscle cells, functioning instead as part of the fibrous skeleton","Sarcolemma is the elastic sheath wrapping each cardiac cell; each muscle layer is wrapped in a fibrous sheet of dense, regular connective tissue (collagen)","Sarcolemma is the heart's electrical conduction pathway, carrying impulses between the SA and AV nodes"],"answer":2,"exp":"The sarcolemma is the elastic sheath wrapping each cardiac cell, and each muscle layer is wrapped in a fibrous sheet made of dense, regular connective tissue (collagen); the fibrous sheets separate superficial from deep layer muscles. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What are the functions of the fibrous skeleton of the heart?","choices":["Stabilizes cardiac cells and heart valves, supports vessels/nerves in the myocardium, distributes contraction forces, prevents overexpansion, provides elastic recoil, and isolates atrial from ventricular cells","Only produces blood cells, supplying the myocardium with a continuous source of new erythrocytes and leukocytes, much like bone marrow does","Only forms the pericardial sac, providing a protective, friction-free outer covering for the heart without any internal structural support","Only conducts electrical impulses, functioning as the sole pathway linking the SA node to the ventricles, without any connective role elsewhere"],"answer":0,"exp":"The fibrous skeleton stabilizes the position of cardiac cells and heart valves, provides support for blood vessels/nerves in the myocardium, helps distribute the forces of contraction, prevents overexpansion of the heart, provides elasticity for recoil after contraction, and isolates atrial cells from ventricular cells. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Where is the heart located, and what vertebral levels does it span in most adults?","choices":["Entirely to the right of midline, spanning C3-C6","Exactly at midline, in the abdominal cavity, spanning L2-L4","In the pelvic cavity, spanning S1 to S5 in most adults","Slightly left of midline, in the mediastinum, spanning ~T4-T8/9"],"answer":3,"exp":"The heart lies slightly to the left of midline, located in the mediastinum, spanning approximately T4 to T8/9 in most adults. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What is the base of the heart, and what is the apex?","choices":["Base is the superior portion; apex is the inferior portion","Base is the inferior portion; apex is the superior portion","Both terms describe the same location","Base and apex both refer to the left ventricle"],"answer":0,"exp":"The base of the heart is the superior portion, and the apex is the inferior portion. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which chamber is the most anterior structure of the heart itself?","choices":["The left ventricle","The left atrium","The right ventricle","The right atrium"],"answer":2,"exp":"The right ventricle is the most anterior structure of the heart itself. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What forms the right border of the heart, and what forms the posterior surface?","choices":["Right border: left atrium; Posterior surface: right ventricle","Both the right border and posterior surface are formed by the same chamber","Right border: right ventricle only; Posterior surface: right atrium","Right border: right atrium only; Posterior surface: left atrium"],"answer":3,"exp":"The right border of the heart is formed by only the right atrium, while the posterior surface is formed by the left atrium. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is the fundamental directional difference between arteries and veins?","choices":["Neither vessel type has a consistent direction of flow","Arteries bring blood toward the heart; veins bring blood away from the heart","Arteries bring blood away from the heart; veins bring blood toward the heart","Both vessel types carry blood in the same direction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Arteries bring blood away from the heart to other parts of the body (systemic circulation), while veins bring blood toward the heart. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What are the smallest arteries called, and what are the smallest veins called?","choices":["Neither vessel type has a specific name for its smallest branches","Arterioles (smallest arteries); venules (smallest veins)","Venules (smallest arteries); arterioles (smallest veins)","Both are called capillaries"],"answer":1,"exp":"The smallest arteries are called arterioles, and the smallest veins are called venules. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Do arteries have pulses? Do veins?","choices":["Veins have pulses; arteries do not","Both have identical, palpable pulses","Neither vessel type has a palpable pulse","Arteries have pulses; veins do not"],"answer":3,"exp":"Arteries have pulses, while veins do not have pulses. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"How do the vessel walls of arteries compare to veins in terms of thickness and smooth muscle?","choices":["Both vessel types have identical wall thickness and smooth muscle content","Neither vessel type contains smooth muscle","Veins have thicker walls with more smooth muscle than arteries","Arteries have thicker walls with more smooth muscle than veins"],"answer":3,"exp":"Arteries have thicker walls than veins, and arteries have more smooth muscle than veins. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"When compressed (e.g., with ultrasound), how do arteries behave compared to veins, and why is this clinically important?","choices":["Arteries maintain their shape when compressed; veins do not — important for procedures like central line placement to differentiate vein from artery","Veins maintain their shape when compressed; arteries collapse instead — irrelevant to central line placement technique","Both vessel types behave identically when compressed, each one fully maintaining its round shape under any amount of applied pressure","Compression has no clinical relevance whatsoever for vessel identification during central line placement"],"answer":0,"exp":"When compressed, arteries maintain their shape while veins do not; this is clinically important, for example when placing a central line in the internal jugular vein (near the carotid artery), to ensure the correct vessel is accessed and the patient does not bleed out. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What are the three layers of vessel walls (except capillaries), from innermost to outermost?","choices":["Endothelium, media, and pericardium","Adventitia, media, intima","Only two layers: media and adventitia","Intima, media, adventitia"],"answer":3,"exp":"The three layers of vessel walls (except capillaries), from innermost to outermost, are the intima (tunica intima), media (tunica media), and adventitia (tunica adventitia). — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is the vasa vasorum?","choices":["A type of heart valve located within the aortic root itself, regulating one-way blood flow","Blood vessels within the walls of the thickest vessels, supplying the vessel wall itself","The innermost layer of a blood vessel, composed entirely of endothelial cells","A type of lymphatic vessel found only within the walls of the thoracic duct"],"answer":1,"exp":"The vasa vasorum are blood vessels contained within the walls of the thickest vessels, supplying the vessel wall itself. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What composes the intima (tunica intima), and what is a key function of the media (tunica media)?","choices":["Both intima and media are composed of identical connective tissue involved in nutrient exchange processes","Intima consists of smooth muscle; media is composed of the vessel's endothelium instead","Neither layer has any described composition or function within the vessel wall itself","Intima is composed of the endothelium; media consists of smooth muscle involved in vasoconstriction and vasodilation"],"answer":3,"exp":"The intima (innermost layer) is composed of the endothelium of the vessel, while the media (middle layer) consists of smooth muscle, involved in vasoconstriction and vasodilation. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What does the adventitia (tunica adventitia) do?","choices":["Composed of the endothelium, lining the vessel lumen","Produces blood cells","Fibers of the adventitia anchor the blood vessels","Consists of smooth muscle controlling vasoconstriction"],"answer":2,"exp":"The adventitia (outermost layer) has fibers that anchor the blood vessels. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is a key difference in the endothelial lining and elastic membranes of arteries versus veins?","choices":["Neither vessel type has any elastic membrane or pleated endothelial folds present in their walls at all","Arteries have pleated endothelial folds and elastic membranes (internal/external) in their intima/media; veins lack both features","Veins have pleated endothelial folds and elastic membranes within their intima/media; arteries lack both of these features","Both vessel types have identical endothelial linings and elastic membranes throughout their intima and media"],"answer":1,"exp":"Arteries have pleated folds in their endothelial lining and thin elastic membranes in their media (external elastic membrane) and intima (internal elastic membrane), while veins lack both of these features. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which vessel type has one-way valves, and why is this clinically relevant to varicose veins?","choices":["Valves are found equally in both the arteries and the veins, and are entirely unrelated to varicose vein development","Both vessel types have identical valve structures, present equally throughout their entire vessel length","Arteries have one-way valves; veins do not, which is why varicose veins never actually develop in veins","Many veins have one-way valves (arteries do not); varicose veins can occur due to weakened walls, damaged valves, or increased blood pressure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Many veins have one-way valves to prevent backflow of blood (arteries do not); varicose veins can occur due to weakened walls, damaged valves, increased blood pressure, and other factors. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What are elastic arteries, and what are examples given?","choices":["Vessels lacking any media layer entirely, found only within the pulmonary circulation as small, thin-walled capillary beds near the alveoli","The smallest arteries, about 30 microns in diameter, controlling blood flow directly into individual capillary beds throughout the tissues","Large vessels up to 2.5 cm in diameter with elastic membranes, tolerating pressure changes; examples include the aorta, brachiocephalic trunk, and pulmonary trunk","Medium-sized vessels up to 0.4 cm in diameter, lacking elastic membranes entirely; examples include the radial and ulnar arteries"],"answer":2,"exp":"Elastic arteries are large vessels up to 2.5 cm in diameter, containing elastic membranes in the media and intima, very resilient and tolerant of pressure changes during the cardiac cycle (recoil during diastole helps propel blood forward); examples include the aorta, brachiocephalic trunk, and pulmonary trunk. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is the main functional difference between the pharynx and larynx?","choices":["Pharynx is a passageway for air only; larynx is a passageway for both air and food","Pharynx is a passageway for air and food; larynx is a passageway for air only and voice production","Both structures serve as identical passageways for air, food, and voice production alike","Neither structure plays any role in swallowing, airway protection, or voice production"],"answer":1,"exp":"The pharynx (throat) is a passageway for both air and food, part of both the respiratory and digestive systems, while the larynx (voice box) is a passageway for air only and is involved in voice production, part of the respiratory system only. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Does the pharynx contain vocal cords? Does the larynx?","choices":["Pharynx contains vocal cords; larynx does not","Neither structure contains any vocal cords at all","Both structures contain a full set of vocal cords","Pharynx does not contain vocal cords; larynx does"],"answer":3,"exp":"The pharynx does not contain vocal cords, while the larynx does contain vocal cords. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What structures make up the upper respiratory system versus the lower respiratory system?","choices":["Upper: trachea, bronchi, bronchioles, and alveoli; Lower: nose, nasal cavity, sinuses, pharynx, and larynx","The larynx is part of the lower respiratory system, grouped with the trachea and bronchi","Upper: nose, nasal cavity, sinuses, pharynx, larynx; Lower: trachea, bronchi, bronchioles, alveoli (inferior to the larynx)","Both divisions include identical structures, each containing the trachea, pharynx, and larynx"],"answer":2,"exp":"The upper respiratory system consists of the nose, nasal cavity, sinuses, pharynx, and larynx; the lower respiratory system consists of structures inferior to the larynx: the trachea, bronchi, bronchioles, and alveoli. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is the difference between the conducting zone and respiratory zone, and how does this differ from the upper/lower respiratory system division?","choices":["Conducting zone (larynx to alveoli) is where gas exchange occurs; respiratory zone (nose to trachea) only moves and cleans air, matching the upper/lower respiratory system division exactly","Conducting and respiratory zones describe the exact same anatomical boundary as the upper and lower respiratory system division, just using different names","Conducting zone (nose to terminal bronchioles) moves/cleans air; respiratory zone (respiratory bronchioles to alveoli) is where gas exchange occurs — this is a different division than upper/lower respiratory system","Both zones are identical to the upper/lower respiratory system division, with the conducting zone matching the upper system exactly in every structure"],"answer":2,"exp":"The conducting zone extends from the nose to the smallest (terminal) bronchioles, moving and cleaning air; the respiratory zone extends from the respiratory bronchioles to the alveoli, where gas exchange occurs; the material explicitly notes this is not the same division as the upper and lower respiratory system. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is pseudostratified, ciliated, columnar epithelium, and where is it found?","choices":["Found only in the pharynx and the thin walls of the alveoli","A type of dense irregular connective tissue, not a true epithelium at all","Found only in the alveoli and the smallest terminal bronchioles themselves","Lines most of the conducting system (except pharynx, smaller bronchi, and alveoli)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Pseudostratified, ciliated, columnar cells line most of the conducting system, except for the pharynx, smaller bronchi, and alveoli. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is the function of ciliated pseudocolumnar cells (\"mucus escalator\"), versus stratified squamous cells?","choices":["Ciliated cells move mucus upward so debris can be coughed out; stratified squamous cells provide protection against abrasion","Both cell types perform an identical function, moving mucus upward so it can be coughed out","Ciliated cells provide protection against abrasion in the pharynx; stratified squamous cells move mucus upward to be coughed out","Neither cell type has any clearly described function within the respiratory tract lining"],"answer":0,"exp":"Ciliated pseudocolumnar cells move mucus in an upward manner (the \"mucus escalator\") so debris can be coughed out, while stratified squamous cells provide protection against abrasion (found in the pharynx). — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What do goblet (mucous) cells produce, and where are they found?","choices":["Produce no secretions; function only in structural support of the ciliated epithelial lining and airway rigidity","Produce mucus so inhaled debris gets stuck and doesn't enter the lungs; found in the nasal cavity and lower respiratory tract","Produce hair-like cilia that beat in rhythmic waves; found only in the trachea and the larynx itself","Produce surfactant that reduces surface tension; found only in the alveoli and respiratory bronchioles"],"answer":1,"exp":"Goblet (mucous) cells produce mucus so that inhaled debris will get stuck and not enter the lungs; they are found in the nasal cavity and the lower respiratory tract. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What are vibrissae, and what stimulates the cough reflex?","choices":["Vibrissae serve no protective function at all; the cough reflex arises spontaneously without any specific trigger","Vibrissae are goblet cells lining the trachea; the cough reflex is stimulated by cranial nerve VII","Vibrissae are hairs in the nose that block inhaled debris; excess mucus stimulates the cough reflex via the vagus nerve (CN X)","Vibrissae are cilia found in the trachea; the cough reflex is stimulated by the phrenic nerve"],"answer":2,"exp":"Vibrissae are hairs in the nose that block some inhaled debris; excess mucus stimulates the cough reflex via the vagus nerve (CN X). — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is the pathway of air through the external nares, nasal vestibule, and nasal cavity, and what role do the conchae play?","choices":["The conchae decrease surface area within the nasal cavity, have no role in warming air, and air bypasses the external nares and vestibule entirely, flowing straight into the pharynx","Air enters external nares, passes the nasal vestibule (surrounded by alar cartilage), enters the nasal cavity, and swirls around the conchae, which increase surface area, trap debris in mucus, and warm the air","Air enters the nasal cavity directly, bypassing the external nares and vestibule entirely, flowing in a straight, unimpeded path with no swirling around the conchae","Air flows in a straight line through the external nares with no swirling around the conchae, which play no role in warming, filtering, or humidifying the air"],"answer":1,"exp":"Air enters the external nares, passes by the nasal vestibule (an area surrounded by two pairs of alar cartilage), and enters the nasal cavity; as air flows in and around the nasal conchae (inferior, middle, superior), debris gets stuck in the mucus, the conchae increase surface area, and the air warms before entering the trachea. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What are the three subdivisions of the pharynx, and their boundaries?","choices":["Nasopharynx (internal nares to soft palate), oropharynx (soft palate to hyoid bone), laryngopharynx (hyoid bone to esophagus entrance)","All three subdivisions share identical boundaries, each spanning from the internal nares down to the esophagus entrance","Nasopharynx (hyoid bone to esophagus), oropharynx (internal nares to soft palate), laryngopharynx (soft palate to hyoid bone)","Only two subdivisions exist: the nasopharynx (internal nares to soft palate) and laryngopharynx (soft palate to esophagus entrance)"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pharynx has three subdivisions: the nasopharynx (where the nose meets the pharynx, internal nares to soft palate), the oropharynx (where the oral cavity meets the pharynx, soft palate to base of tongue/hyoid bone), and the laryngopharynx (entrance to the trachea and esophagus, hyoid bone to entrance of esophagus). — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"At what vertebral levels does the larynx begin and end?","choices":["Begins at C6; ends at T1","Begins at C1; ends at C3","Begins at C3 or C4; ends at C6","Begins at T1; ends at T4"],"answer":2,"exp":"The larynx begins at the level of vertebra C3 or C4 and ends at the level of vertebra C6. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"How many total cartilages make up the larynx, and how are they divided into unpaired versus paired?","choices":["9 total cartilages: 3 unpaired (single) and 3 paired (6 structures)","12 total cartilages: 6 unpaired and 6 paired structures total","6 total cartilages: all unpaired, with none paired at all","3 total cartilages total, all of them paired structures"],"answer":0,"exp":"The larynx contains 9 cartilages total: 3 unpaired (single) cartilages and 3 paired cartilages (6 structures total). — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 24"}]};
